Given that $-{ }^n C_4,{ }^n C_5$ and ${ }^n C_6$ are in A.P
$\begin{aligned}
& \therefore 2^n C_5={ }^n C_4+{ }^n C_6 \\
& \Rightarrow 2 \cdot \frac{n!}{(n-5)!5!}=\frac{n!}{(n-4)!4!}+\frac{n!}{(n-6)!6!}
\end{aligned}\begin{gathered}\Rightarrow \frac{2 n!}{5(n-5)(n-6)!4!}=\frac{n!}{(n-4)(n-5)(n-6)!4!}+\frac{n!}{(n-6)!6.5!4!} \\ \Rightarrow \frac{2 n!}{5(n-5)(n-6)!4!}=\frac{n!}{(n-6)!4!} \\ {\left[\frac{1}{(n-4)(n-5)}+\frac{1}{6.5}\right]}\end{gathered}\begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow \frac{2}{(n-5)(n-6)}=\frac{n^2-9 n+50}{6(n-4)(n-5)} \\ & \Rightarrow 12(n-4)=(n-5)\left(n^2-9 n+50\right) \\ & \Rightarrow n^3-15 n^2+92 n-252=0 \\ & \Rightarrow n^3-7 n^2-8 n^2+56 n+36 n-252=0 \\ & \Rightarrow n^2(n-7)-8 n(n-7)+36(n-7)=0 \\ & \Rightarrow(n-7)\left(n^2-8 n+36\right)=0 \\ & \Rightarrow n=7\left[\therefore \text { roots of } n^2-8 n+36 \text { is not real }\right]\end{aligned}$

questions.q:
The marks obtained by 60 students in a certain subject out of 75 are given below :
\begin{array}{|cc|}\hline Marks & Number of Students \\\hline 15-20 & 4 \\20-25 & 5 \\25-30 & 11 \\30-35 & 6 \\35-40 & 5 \\40-45 & 8 \\45-50 & 9 \\50-55 & 6 \\55-60 & 4 \\60-65 & 2 \\\hline\end{array}
What is the median?

questions.solution:
$\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\hline Marks & Midpoint & f & c f \\\hline 15-20 & 17.5 & 4 & 4 \\\hline 20-25 & 22.5 & 5 & 9 \\\hline 25-30 & 27.5 & 11 & 20 \\\hline 30-35 & 32.5 & 6 & 26 \\\hline 35-40 & 37.5 & 5 & 31 \\\hline 40-45 & 42.5 & 8 & 39 \\\hline 45-50 & 47.5 & 9 & 48 \\\hline 50-55 & 52.5 & 6 & 54 \\\hline 55-60 & 57.5 & 4 & 58 \\\hline 60-65 & 62.5 & 2 & 60 \\\hline\end{array}
$n=60$
- The class interval containing $\frac{n^{\text {th }}}{2}$ or $30^{\text {th }}$ item is 35-40. Therefore, $35-40$ is the median class. We know that
$\begin{aligned}
& \text { Median }=l+\frac{\frac{n}{2}-c}{f} \times h \\
& \text { Here, } l=35, n=60, f=5, h=5, c=26 \\
& \therefore \text { Median }=35+\frac{30-26}{5} \times 5 \\
& \mathrm{M}=39
\end{aligned}

